Trading peer-to-peer (P2P) using MTN MoMo is the most popular way to buy and sell crypto in the XAF zone (Cameroon, Gabon, etc.). [1, 2] 
Because it is instant and mobile, it requires specific security precautions during P2P transactions: [3] 
## 🟢 When Buying Crypto with MTN MoMo
You are sending MoMo to get crypto. [4] 

   1. Initiate the Trade: Choose a merchant on a platform like [Binance P2P](https://p2p.binance.com/trade/MTNMobileMoney/USDT?fiat=XAF) or [NoOnes](https://blog.noones.com/binance-p2p-alternative-cameroon) accepting MTN MoMo. [4, 5] 
   2. Send the Money: Copy the merchant's precise mobile phone number and name from the order page. Open your phone dialer, use your local code (e.g., *126# in Cameroon), and transfer the exact XAF amount. [6] 
   3. Double Check Names: The name on the MTN MoMo prompt must match the verified name of the merchant on the P2P platform. Do not send money to a third-party account.
   4. Notify the Seller: Hit the "Transferred, notify seller" button on the app only after you have safely completed the transaction. [5, 6] 

## 🔴 When Selling Crypto for MTN MoMo
You are receiving MoMo in exchange for your crypto. [4] 

* 
* The "Wrong Number Transfer" Scam: A massive scam trend involves a fraudster buying your crypto and sending you real MoMo. As soon as you release the crypto, they call MTN Support claiming they "mistakenly sent money to the wrong number" to initiate a reversal. You end up losing both your crypto and your XAF. [7] 
* The Defense: When selling, the moment you receive the MoMo confirmation SMS, immediately withdraw the cash at a nearby agent or transfer it to a secondary wallet/bank account before clicking "Payment Received" to release the crypto. [7] 
* 

## ⛔ Vital Golden Rules for MoMo P2P

* 
* Ignore SMS Spoofing: Scammers can use fake SMS gateways to send an alert that looks like an official MTN MoMo deposit. Always check your actual balance balance code (*126# or via the MoMo app) to verify the funds are physically inside your account.
* No "Urgency" Pressure: If a buyer sends a fake screenshot and pressures you via the P2P chat box saying "My phone is dying, please release fast," ignore them. If the funds aren't in your balance, do not click release. [7, 8, 9] 
* 

